<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (I admit that I have issues with insecurity. I'm constantly afraid that things that I like will go away, and that is a fear has been re-enforced over the years with the great things that we've all lost. This anxiety applies to Stacker News as well and I've had the lifelong conviction that if you aren't growing, you're dying; a quote that I heard my dad shout from a pulpit of our church when I was twelve or thirteen. I still hear that line in the back of my head when I look at communitites that I've become a part of.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
164 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (I like SN. It's one of the few places on the internet that has a good incentive structure and on top of that, an incentive structure that ties into bitcoin. I haven't been here long and I'm also pretty late to the party so my long-term experience isn't really there. I'm sure that there's a lot that I've missed because of that. Regardless, I feel a connection to this place and want it to succeed.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
165 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (A part of that desire to see this place succeed is growth. If I could, I would want to see a million people here, zapping around and arguing but that isn't necessarily a good thing. Places lose part of what made them great when huge crowds show up. Generally speaking, the more people who show up, the lower the average quality. That million people might just be the worst thing that could happen here compared to a tiny trickle of new users or even losing users. I can understand why someone would want this place to stay small.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
166 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 141 0 R
/E (Would that happen here? Would seeing huge growth across Stacker News ruin it? If not, how do we grow? If the path ahead for this place is growth, how do we ensure the people that we draw here are the kind of people we would want to bring in? I know this is a bit odd for someone who hasn't managed to be here for a full year, but this really does bother me.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [9]
>>
